"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including a preoperative PSA of 5.2 and an ISUP grade group 2 pathology from the prostatectomy specimen. Favorable findings include negative surgical margins, no extraprostatic extension, and no seminal vesicle invasion documented in the operative report. The absence of removed lymph nodes widens uncertainty regarding nodal status. Biopsy findings showed Gleason 3+4 and 3+3 patterns, which differ slightly from the primary tumor grade noted post-surgery (Gleason 3+4).\n\nThe trajectory of postoperative PSA is unknown, and follow-up data are unavailable, limiting prognostic assessment. The lack of nodal sampling means that pNx status cannot be definitively interpreted as pN0. Future information regarding the postoperative PSA level and its subsequent pattern would significantly revise this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 84.76 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 50.86–118.66 months; its coverage has not been validated."
